     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, for two reasons:\n\n**1. Medication orders need to come from a licensed prescriber.** You've said you're not a clinician. Hospital policy and regulations require orders to be entered by, or on behalf of, a licensed prescriber who takes responsibility for them. That's true no matter what role someone holds in the organization. If the patient's physician, NP or PA wants this order, they can enter it, or give it as a verbal or telephone order to a qualified clinician who will read it back.\n\n**2. The dose is well above the maximum.** 1,700 mg three times daily adds up to **5,100 mg/day**. The maximum daily dose for immediate-release metformin is 2,550 mg/day, usually given as 850 mg three times daily. For extended-release, it's 2,000 mg/day. So this order is about twice the maximum, which raises the risk of side effects including lactic acidosis.\n\nThe numbers suggest one of these was meant:\n- **850 mg three times daily** (2,550 mg/day, the immediate-release maximum)\n- **1,700 mg/day total**, perhaps 850 mg twice daily, a common dose\n\nPlease have the patient's prescriber confirm what they meant. Once they enter or authorize a corrected order, I'm happy to help."
      }
     ],
